-- title:
--   Degree of a Frobenius endomorphism: [F:φⁿ(F)]=(p^r)ⁿ
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a perfect field and $F$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and let $p$ be a prime with $K$ of characteristic $p$. Assume $F$ is a one-variable function field over $K$ in the sense that there exists $t \in F$ transcendental over $K$ for which $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(t)$ generated by $t$. Let $\varphi \colon F \to F$ be a $K$-algebra homomorphism satisfying [`AlgebraicCurve.IsFrobeniusEndo (p ^ r)`](def/AlgebraicCurve_FrobeniusEndo.html#L16) for a natural number $r$, i.e. for every $x \in F$ there is $y \in F$ with $\varphi(y) = x^{p^r}$, and for every $y \in F$ there is $x \in F$ with $\varphi(y) = x^{p^r}$ (so the image of $\varphi$ is exactly the subfield of $p^r$-th powers). Then for every natural number $n$, the quantity [`AlgebraicCurve.finrankAlong K (φ ^ n)`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Correspondence.html#L51) — by definition the $F$-dimension of $F$ when the source copy acts on the target copy through the $n$-fold composite $\varphi^n$, that is $[F : \varphi^n(F)]$ — equals $(p^r)^n$. For $n = 0$ this reads $1 = 1$, and for $n = 1$ it gives $[F:\varphi(F)] = p^r$.
--
--   This is the statement that a Frobenius endomorphism of exponent $q = p^r$ of a one-variable function field is purely inseparable of degree $q$, and that its iterates have degree $q^n$ — the function-field form of the assertion that the $q$-power Frobenius morphism of a curve has degree $q$. It supplies the degree input for later identities relating traces and degrees of correspondences on curves.
